Mamba "align" prefix caching: keep the conversation's reachable state
snapshots alive until request end (issue #52; upstream vllm#45238).

A hybrid prefix-cache hit is the INTERSECTION of per-group hits
(HybridKVCacheCoordinator.find_longest_cache_hit): the mamba group can only
resume from a retained state snapshot, so losing the snapshots inside the
shared prefix discards the whole attention-group match. Prefix caching then
reports a clean 0% on a conversation whose attention blocks are 100% cached,
and the turn re-prefills from scratch.

Three stock behaviors compose into exactly the periodic TTFT spikes of
issue #52 (and the upstream thread):

  1. GEOMETRY: align mode materializes roughly one usable prompt-region
     snapshot per turn, at the last prefill chunk-step boundary (~2048-token
     grid, aligned down to the 448-token mamba block). The coordinator only
     accepts a mamba snapshot at or below the attention match minus one
     EAGLE margin (448 tokens with spec decode), so on the ~22% of turns
     (448/2048) where the snapshot lands inside that margin, the turn needs
     the PREVIOUS generation's snapshot instead. That frequency — roughly
     one turn in 4-5 — is the reported period.
  2. CoW RELEASE: the previous generation's snapshot is exactly the block a
     turn's partial hit resumes from. The hit CoWs the state and releases
     the cached copy right after the copy runs, EARLY in the turn — so by
     turn end the chain's most valuable block is one of the oldest free
     blocks in the pool.
  3. EVICTION ORDER: remove_skipped_blocks frees the newly written boundary
     snapshots mid-decode too. Net: every block a follow-up turn could
     resume from sits near the head of the free queue, while the attention
     blocks they unlock sit safely at the tail.

Any concurrent traffic between turns then evicts the snapshots first; a
geometry-unlucky turn with the fallback gone reconciles to 0. Reproduced on
syv (RTX 3090, dflash2, PREFIX_CACHE=1, pool shrunk to 32.6k tokens): a
12-turn conversation with two unrelated 3.4k-token requests interleaved
between turns holds 81-91% hits for five turns, then 0% on EVERY turn —
TTFT 2.1 s -> 17-31 s, each a full re-prefill — with the instrumented
lookup showing the coordinator finding and discarding a 16,576-token
attention match (num_uncached_common_prefix_tokens = 16576).

The fix (opt-in, VLLM_MAMBA_ALIGN_KEEP_CHECKPOINTS=1) retains, per running
request and mamba group, up to THREE state blocks until the request finishes — the CoW-carried key it resumed from and
the last written prompt-region snapshots (rolling; a fourth frees the
oldest) — and frees them after everything else, giving them the freshest
eviction position in the pool. It reorders and briefly holds blocks the
request already owns; hit semantics, scheduling and model output are
unchanged.

Two stronger variants were built, measured, and deliberately NOT shipped:
pinning every hashed mamba page starved the attention pool outright, and a
bounded evict-last rotation in the allocator displaced each skipped
eviction onto the conversation's own attention tail, collapsing the very
hit it protected. The unit of value is the attention chain PLUS its
snapshot; when between-turn traffic exceeds the whole free pool, nothing
survives by policy — that regime is capacity sizing, not eviction order.

Validation (this patch):
  - Failure cell (12-turn conversation, two interleaved 3.4k-token requests
    per gap, pool shrunk to 32.6k tokens): 0% from turn 6 on stock code,
    TTFT 17-31 s per turn. That cell turned out to be capacity-bound — the
    interleaves' per-gap block churn exceeds the entire free pool, so no
    eviction policy holds it (five variants and stock all fail identically
    there). It is where the diagnosis came from, not what the patch targets.
  - No-harm A/B at two realistic pool sizes (4.2 GB and 5.6 GB, 12 turns,
    interleaved traffic): with retention enabled, hit rate and TTFT are
    identical to stock turn-for-turn (82-92% hits, 1.9-2.9 s TTFT).
  - The win regime is the reported one — context comparable to the pool,
    light background traffic, enough turns for the ~22% geometry lottery —
    which a short cell cannot cheaply occupy. On such a deployment the
    periodic 0% turns are predicted to become one-generation-shorter hits.
    The patch ships DEFAULT OFF: enable with
    VLLM_MAMBA_ALIGN_KEEP_CHECKPOINTS=1 and please report either way.

Apply from the installed vllm package directory:
  patch -p1 -d venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vllm < patches/mamba-align-checkpoint-order.patch

Written against vLLM 0.27.1.

